{"has_more_entries": true, "subscriptions": "", "error": "", "posts": [{"is_locked": false, "tweet_id": "2111159463088447526", "sticky_comment": 0, "is_video_mp4": false, "type": "link", "uuid": "1ATVjYVFLT", "is_twitter": true, "is_admin": false, "score": 18, "suggested_sort": 0, "post_flair_class": "", "id": 8890447, "raw_content": "", "author": "TrumpCare", "created": 1792232597273, "profile_picture": "", "community": "TheDonald", "ad_tier": 2, "last_comment_author": "GEOTUSdaGOATUS", "is_video": false, "video_link": "", "is_new_user": false, "domain": "twitter.com", "is_crosspost": false, "preview": "https://media.scored.co/preview/1ATVjYVFLT.png", "removal_source": "", "link": "https://twitter.com/Polymarket/status/2111159463088447526", "author_flair_class": "", "is_removed": false, "media": {"resized_resolutions": [], "blurhash": "", "full_resolution": []}, "title": "NEW POLYMARKET: Next Press Secretary?\r\n", "last_comment_created": 1792240832227, "content": "", "score_down": 0, "author_flair_text": "", "pro_tier": 0, "is_stickied": false, "is_nsfw": false, "is_deleted": false, "is_image": false, "comments": 4, "score_up": 18, "is_edited": false, "is_moderator": false, "awards": 0, "vote_state": 2, "link_metadata": {"twitter": {"quotedTweet": false, "images": false, "linkEmbed": {"image": "https://pbs.twimg.com/card_img/2111159448316092416/AnGMNUQm?format=png&name=800x419", "domain": "polymarket.com", "description": "View real-time odds for \"Next Press Secretary?\" as of October 16, 2026 and trade on The World\u2019s Largest Prediction Market\u2122", "title": "Next Press Secretary?", "url": "https://t.co/5S09QZ4y5L"}, "replies": 54, "created": 1792174614000, "id": "2111159463088447526", "video": false, "poll": false, "user": {"profilePicture": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2005664281002491904/bz2ZO_nU_normal.jpg", "displayName": "Polymarket", "username": "Polymarket"}, "content": "\ud83d\udea8 NEW POLYMARKET: Next Press Secretary?\n\n", "parentTweet": false, "likes": 176}}, "post_flair_text": "", "archive_id": ""}, {"is_locked": false, "tweet_id": "", "sticky_comment": 0, "is_video_mp4": false, "type": "text", "uuid": "1ATVjYVF8z", "is_twitter": false, "is_admin": false, "score": 22, "suggested_sort": 0, "post_flair_class": "", "id": 8890411, "raw_content": "\"what year will baby boomers become just 1% of the US population?\"\n\nCopilot Search \n\nYear Baby Boomers Will Be 1% of the U.S. Population\n\nBased on U.S. Census Bureau projections, Baby Boomers are expected to make up about 1% of the U.S. population around the year 2080.\n\nHow this is calculated\n\nBaby Boomers are defined as people born between 1946 and 1964. As of 2026, there are roughly 66 million Boomers living in the United States SavingAdvice.com BlogSavingAdvice.com Blog. \n\nCensus Bureau projections show that the U.S. population will continue to grow slowly but steadily, reaching about 6.6 billion people by 2080 Census.govCensus.gov.\n\nIf the number of Boomers remains roughly constant (accounting for deaths and limited immigration of Boomers), then:\n\nShare of Boomers=Number of BoomersTotal U.S. Population\nShare\u224866 million6.6 billion\u22480.01 (or 1%)\n\nThis means that by the time the U.S. population reaches about 6.6 billion, Boomers will represent roughly 1% of the total.\n\nContext from projections\n\n    Current share (2026): ~1% of the U.S. population (66 million out of ~6.6 billion) SavingAdvice.com BlogSavingAdvice.com Blog.\n\n    By 2030: All Boomers will be over 65, and older adults will make up about 20% of the population Census.govCensus.gov.\n\n    By 2060: Nearly 25% of Americans will be 65 or older, but the Boomers\u2019 share will be much smaller because the total population will be far larger Census.govCensus.gov.\n\n    By ~2080: The Boomers\u2019 share will drop to about 1% as the U.S. population grows to ~6.6 billion Census.govCensus.gov+1.\n\nWhy the share shrinks\n\nEven though the Boomers\u2019 absolute number stays roughly the same, the U.S. population is projected to grow due to immigration and slower natural decline, so the proportion of Boomers in the total population will steadily decline.\n\nIn short: Baby Boomers are already about 1% of the U.S. population today, and they will remain at roughly that level through the late 21st century, with the exact 1% mark occurring around 2080 under current projections.\n\nhttps://www.census.gov/library/publications/2020/demo/p25-1144.html", "author": "Lunar", "created": 1792228520759, "profile_picture": "AAVSlrgdPsSU", "community": "TheDonald", "ad_tier": 2, "last_comment_author": "Abovethefray", "is_video": false, "video_link": "", "is_new_user": false, "domain": "", "is_crosspost": false, "preview": "", "removal_source": "", "link": "", "author_flair_class": "", "is_removed": false, "media": {"resized_resolutions": [], "blurhash": "", "full_resolution": []}, "title": "I asked an AI a simple question about the Boomer mortality rate. But since the AI was trained on Boomer Data/ it thinks like a Boomer/ & gave the most Boomer answer: For every American Boomer who dies-- they're going to import a foreign-shithole boomer-vampire to replace them & live forever...", "last_comment_created": 1792245709864, "content": "<p>&quot;what year will baby boomers become just 1% of the US population?&quot;</p>\n<p>Copilot Search</p>\n<p>Year Baby Boomers Will Be 1% of the U.S. Population</p>\n<p>Based on U.S. Census Bureau projections, Baby Boomers are expected to make up about 1% of the U.S. population around the year 2080.</p>\n<p>How this is calculated</p>\n<p>Baby Boomers are defined as people born between 1946 and 1964. As of 2026, there are roughly 66 million Boomers living in the United States SavingAdvice.com BlogSavingAdvice.com Blog.</p>\n<p>Census Bureau projections show that the U.S. population will continue to grow slowly but steadily, reaching about 6.6 billion people by 2080 Census.govCensus.gov.</p>\n<p>If the number of Boomers remains roughly constant (accounting for deaths and limited immigration of Boomers), then:</p>\n<p>Share of Boomers=Number of BoomersTotal U.S. Population\nShare\u224866 million6.6 billion\u22480.01 (or 1%)</p>\n<p>This means that by the time the U.S. population reaches about 6.6 billion, Boomers will represent roughly 1% of the total.</p>\n<p>Context from projections</p>\n<pre><code>Current share (2026): ~1% of the U.S. population (66 million out of ~6.6 billion) SavingAdvice.com BlogSavingAdvice.com Blog.\n\nBy 2030: All Boomers will be over 65, and older adults will make up about 20% of the population Census.govCensus.gov.\n\nBy 2060: Nearly 25% of Americans will be 65 or older, but the Boomers\u2019 share will be much smaller because the total population will be far larger Census.govCensus.gov.\n\nBy ~2080: The Boomers\u2019 share will drop to about 1% as the U.S. population grows to ~6.6 billion Census.govCensus.gov+1.\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why the share shrinks</p>\n<p>Even though the Boomers\u2019 absolute number stays roughly the same, the U.S. population is projected to grow due to immigration and slower natural decline, so the proportion of Boomers in the total population will steadily decline.</p>\n<p>In short: Baby Boomers are already about 1% of the U.S. population today, and they will remain at roughly that level through the late 21st century, with the exact 1% mark occurring around 2080 under current projections.</p>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2020/demo/p25-1144.html\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.census.gov/library/publications/2020/demo/p25-1144.html</a></p>\n", "score_down": 0, "author_flair_text": "", "pro_tier": 0, "is_stickied": false, "is_nsfw": false, "is_deleted": false, "is_image": false, "comments": 11, "score_up": 22, "is_edited": false, "is_moderator": false, "awards": 0, "vote_state": 2, "link_metadata": {"status": 200}, "post_flair_text": "", "archive_id": ""}, {"is_locked": false, "tweet_i